            <div xml:id="d1057">

                <pb facs="1050" n="1038" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg1038"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.62114 Mail/146: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-19">June 19,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received June 20—3:34 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3928. American Interests—Far East. POW and internee mail, Department’s 1851, May
                        29.<anchor n="68" xml:id="d1057-f1"/> Foreign Office note June 17 states
                    Swiss Legation, Tokyo, replies to contents Department 1379, April 21<anchor
                        n="69" xml:id="d1057-f2"/> that Japanese authorities accept in principle
                    Department’s proposal. However, Ministry of Communications feels mail from Japan
                    should be sent to Moscow for retransmission Tehran by Soviet authorities.
                    Director Japanese post wrote in foregoing sense May 27 to Commissaire [of]
                    Postal Communications, Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
